    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M5 V23: Escape velocity. What minimum launch speed allows escape without further propulsion? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "Think about its energy. With gravity the only force, its total energy, kinetic plus potential, stays the same. As it climbs, kinetic energy turns into potential energy, so it slows down. Each extra kilometre costs less than the one before, because the pull keeps weakening. If its kinetic energy runs out before it gets far enough, it stops and falls back. But remember, potential energy only rises to zero, even at infinity. So the probe needs only enough kinetic energy to cover that finite climb, not an infinite amount. The slowest escape is when the probe just reaches infinity, with zero speed left. Out there, its kinetic energy is zero, and its potential energy is zero, so its total energy is zero. Total energy doesn't change, so it must be zero at launch too. At launch, that's a half m v squared, minus big G M m over r, equals zero. It's like having exactly enough to pay for the whole climb, with nothing left over. The mass m cancels. So v squared equals two big G M over r, and the escape velocity is the square root of two big G M over r. From Earth's surface, with the data sheet values, that's the square root of two times four point zero zero two times ten to the fourteen, over six point three seven one times ten to the six. That gives about one point one two times ten to the four metres per second, or eleven point two kilometres per second. NASA quotes the same value, eleven point two. That's about forty thousand kilometres an hour. From the Moon, with far less mass, it's only about two point four.",
