    "openingDecision": "opens on v22's final frame (see \"Opening frame\"); the energy graph shrinks to the top right. Vertical launch stage (kit gap 8) with the live launch-speed control (see \"Simulation\"): the satellite plate (`y12p-satellite-v1.png`, here \"probe\") leaves the painted Earth (`phys-y11-earth-v1.png`) straight up with a teal readout (52 px) \"launch speed: 5 km s⁻¹\" and a live height readout; it rises (time compressed, clock readout), stops at a height marker \"h = 1.6 × 10⁶ m\" and falls back; a grey ghost marker sits at \"mgh estimate: 1.3 × 10⁶ m\" (tag \"v21: constant g underestimates the climb\"). On \"back\" the slider moves to \"10 km s⁻¹\" and a second launch rises far higher (Earth scaled down to fit), marker \"h = 2.5 × 10⁷ m\", and falls back. Hand tags (30 px): teal \"faster\", grey \"still falls back\", and a grey pen ring round the two height markers. On \"think\" the 3 s `Countdown` runs beside \"Never comes back: at what speed? Infinite?\" with three option chips (36 px) \"infinite\", \"a finite speed\", \"no such speed\".",
    "understandingCheck": "Your turn. Mars has a mass of six point four two times ten to the twenty-three kilograms, and a radius of three point three nine times ten to the six metres. Calculate the escape velocity from its surface, and state one assumption you made. Pause here to answer. Last one. A satellite is in a circular orbit around a planet. Its engine fires briefly to increase its speed. By what factor must its speed increase, so that it can escape with no further propulsion? Pause here to answer.",
